fips-initramfs (0.1.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Initial release. Starts a FIPS mesh node in the initramfs and binds
    dropbear to its fips0 address, so an encrypted root filesystem can be
    unlocked over the mesh rather than over the local IP network. Console
    passphrase entry is never disabled.
  * The package carries the fips daemon and fipsctl, taken from a published
    FIPS release at build time and verified against that release's
    checksums, so installing this one package is the whole procedure.
    /usr/share/fips-initramfs/fips-version records which release. A machine
    that also installs the fips package uses /usr/bin first.
  * The install asks two questions: the SSH public key allowed to unlock the
    machine, written to /etc/dropbear/initramfs/authorized_keys, and a
    bootstrap peer. Neither is asked again on a second install, which keeps
    the identity and so keeps the mesh address. To see what a first install
    does, purge first.
  * An SSH session into the initramfs runs cryptroot-unlock and cannot open
    a shell. The forced command is added to the copy of authorized_keys
    inside the generated image, so the file in /etc/dropbear/initramfs stays
    untouched. Set FIPS_FORCE_UNLOCK_COMMAND=no to turn it off during
    bring-up.
  * /etc/fips-initramfs/fips.yaml is created by the install rather than
    shipped as a conffile, so an upgrade never produces a merge prompt for
    it and never overwrites an edited file. The only conffile is
    fips-initramfs.conf. Purging removes the directory and the identity
    with it.
  * The full list of changes in this release is in the project changelog,
    shipped at /usr/share/doc/fips-initramfs/CHANGELOG.md.gz. This file
    carries only what is specific to the Debian packaging.

 -- Johnathan Corgan <johnathan@corganlabs.com>  Tue, 01 Sep 2026 07:35:06 +0000
